## THE DECISIONS INSIDE THIS LANE (each Tuesday's default as coordinator; each a Question at the stamp; each a behaviour with a cell)
- **D1 FIVE COMMITS, ONE BRANCH, ONE PUSH (Q1).** C1 to C5 in the order of THE WORK, each green on its own (each sets `FLOOR` to its own count: 60, 71, 76, 76, 84), one push of the head. Alternative: one lane commit. Cost of the default: none at landing (squash makes one commit either way); the gate reads five diffs in place of one.
- **D2 R6, THE WORKFLOW SPELLINGS (Q2).** Outside a block scalar, a line under `.github/workflows/` holding a quoted mapping key (`"k":` or `'k':`), an explicit key (`? `), ANY backslash, or a `{` outside a `${{ }}` expression is a finding; a block scalar with an indentation number (`|2`) is a finding; a line naming `BASH_ENV` in any case is a finding, inside a block or not. A block scalar runs from a key ending `|` or `>` (with `-` or `+`) to the first non-blank line indented less than the block's first line; a block whose first line is not indented more than its key's column is empty. The ticket asks for "any backslash escape inside double quotes"; the default refuses every backslash outside a block (a double-quoted scalar can continue on a line that holds no quote). Alternative: backslashes inside a double-quoted segment only. Cost of the default: a harmless backslash in a single-quoted value is refused (0 on BASE, MEASURED). The explicit key and the indentation number are additions: each is a way to write a key or a block the line reader would misread.
- **D3 R7, THE LIST (Q3, STOP-class).** `R7_LISTED = ()` in `build/repo_rules.py`: no path is listed, because no tracked file of BASE or of any branch the origin holds has a NUL byte (MEASURED, 14 refs, control fired). Alternative: list paths now (none needs it). **Cost: any later lane that tracks a binary fixture or a UTF-16 text file goes red on `rules` and must ask Tuesday, because `build/**` is this lane's.**
- **D4 R8, ANY FILTER ATTRIBUTE (Q4).** A tracked `.gitattributes` (any folder) whose line, not a `#` comment, gives a path `filter=<any driver>` or the bare word `filter`, in any case, is a finding; `-filter` and `!filter` (unset) are not. Alternative: `filter=lfs` only. Cost of the default: none today (the two tracked `.gitattributes` read `* -text`, MEASURED).
- **D5 THE WHOLE-FILE FINGERPRINT (Q5).** `REVIEWED_WORKFLOW` = SHA-256 of `ci.yml`'s bytes with the number after `FLOOR=` read as `N`; the two job fingerprints stay. A third new case requires `ci.yml` to be the only file under `.github/workflows/`. The README says how a build adds a job and explains the two former false reds (a job added after `rules`, a top-level block moved below `jobs:`): they now also move the whole-file fingerprint, which a build sets in the same commit. Alternative: replace the two job fingerprints by the whole-file one. Cost of the default: an edit inside a fingerprinted job sets two constants.
- **D6 THE COPY CHECKS (Q6).** In `neutral_export`, after the copies are written, one `find ... -exec wc -c {} + | awk` pass requires for EVERY listed object both copies; the second copy 43008 bytes longer than the first; the first copy of a blob, commit or tag exactly 55 bytes longer than the object (`%(objectsize)`); a tree's first copy longer than 55 bytes exactly when the tree's size is not 0. It prints `NEUTRAL-EXPORT: listed <n>, copies of the right length <2n>, wrong 0` and fails the step otherwise. The 43008 check is beyond the ticket: it closes the review's "the block of the second copy cut to one line" balance. Alternative: the 55-byte check only. Cost: one `find` and one `awk` process per export.
- **D7 THE REACH CHECK (Q7).** After the existing `NEUTRAL-SCAN:` line (unchanged), `git rev-list --objects --all` must name no object absent from the listing; it prints `NEUTRAL-SCAN-REACH: objects a ref of the checkout reaches <r>, not among the stored objects 0`. Without it, the ticket's acceptance "a listing that leaves out an object of another branch turns it red" is not met (the 55-byte check cannot see an object that is not listed). Alternative: none that meets the acceptance.
- **D8 ONE PLANTED REPOSITORY PER PLACE: NOT BUILT (Q8).** It would change the line `PLANTED-CONTROL neutral-scan: planted in 11 places, found by name 1, found under a neutral name 25 of 25, rc 1`, which every live seat's hosted reader requires verbatim. D6 already turns the review's two measured balances red (MEASURED: rows `first-line-cut`, `block-cut` of THE CONTROLS). Alternative: build it and carry a new reader line to every live kit.
- **D9 ONE BATCH PROCESS FOR THE EXPORT: NOT BUILT (Q9).** The step took 11 s locally for 909 objects (MEASURED, 20:23:21 to 20:23:38 for two steps); the hosted secret-scan job of BASE took 21 s whole (RELAYED from its run's start and end times). Alternative: `git cat-file --batch` in one process. Cost: none at today's size.
- **D10 THE SQUASH SCAN'S SHAPE (Q10).** One run does the CONTROL first (the message plus the line `value = "<planted value>"`: exit code 1 and at least 2 findings, one per copy of the commit) and then the MESSAGE (exit code 0, 0 findings); git runs with no global or system configuration, no hook, `--cleanup=verbatim`; the message is the subject, a blank line and the body (the subject alone when the body file is empty); the scratch folder must lie outside the checkout. `--scanner` defaults to `gitleaks` on `PATH`. A message with a finding prints `SQUASH-SCAN: FINDING in the message` before any control verdict. Alternative: control and message as separate runs. Its cells run against a stand-in scanner because the `rules` job has no scanner (D13).
- **D11 UNOPENED FORMATS ARE G3 IN EVERY MODE (Q11).** A file whose first bytes are gzip, bzip2, xz, zstd, 7z, rar or cab, or that holds `ustar` at offset 257 (tar), is a G3 finding, inside a ZIP too. Alternative: open gzip, bzip2, xz and tar with the standard library and judge their members. **Cost of the default: a later publish shape with compressed static files (for example `.gz` web assets of a future console) turns the leg's guard red until the guard is extended: loud, never silent.** Brotli has no magic bytes and is not seen (a named limit).
- **D12 KEY STORES IN RELEASE MODE ONLY (Q11).** In release mode a file named `*.pfx`, `*.p12`, `*.jks`, `*.jceks`, `*.keystore`, `*.bks`, `*.uber`, or starting with a Java key store's magic, or with a PKCS#12 head, is G3. Not in default mode ("a finding in a release artefact", the ticket). Today's legs run default mode: unchanged.
- **D13 THE CODE IS THE DRAFTER'S, APPLIED BY YOU (Q12).** The seven diffs, in order, each after `git apply --check`, the suite after each; tests first where a rule is new (C2, C5).
- **D14 THE WORD SCAN'S ALLOWANCE (Q12).** `words2.py` = `words.py` plus one allowance: it removes Python byte escapes `\xNN` (read as seat ids: `\x28`) and the repository's own names `REVIEWED_SCAN_JOB`, `REVIEWED_RULES_JOB`, `REVIEWED_WORKFLOW` and the phrase "reviewed text" (on BASE since the CI skeleton) before counting, and prints how many it removed. Without it, `words.py` prints `WORDS: 20 hits` on the added lines (10 escapes, 10 of that name family; MEASURED). Its control still fires 13 of 13 and still counts "after the review" and "X26" after the allowance.

